<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Network security config for the Bebok Android app.

  HISTORY
  - F10-10 (WP-M3) introduced this file with `cleartextTrafficPermitted="false"`
    as the base policy and an explicit allow-list of the only cleartext hosts
    that package used: the embedded engine on loopback and the emulator's host
    alias. It also documented the limitation below.
  - F10-28 (WP-M6) switched the base policy to `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"`.
    Read the decision before "fixing" it back.

  DECISION (F10-28, PLAN-1.6-MOBILE.md section 5, WP-M6 brief)
  Remote mode reaches a desktop engine over plain HTTP at an address that is
  only known at pairing time: a Tailscale CGNAT address (100.64.0.0/10) or,
  when the user deliberately enables `remote.allow_lan`, an RFC1918 LAN
  address. Android's static Network Security Config has no CIDR / range
  syntax - `<domain>` entries are exact hostname or IP-literal matches only
  (documented AOSP limitation) - so "allow cleartext to private and tailnet
  ranges, deny it to the internet" cannot be expressed here. Two options were
  weighed:

    (A) HTTPS with the engine's self-signed certificate pinned at pairing.
        Needs TLS termination in the engine's remote listener plus client
        pinning. Rejected for 1.6: engine changes are out of WP-M6's scope and
        the plan schedules `tailscale cert` + rustls for 1.7 (section 9, risk 8).
        That is the upgrade path: once the listener serves TLS, the base policy
        below can return to `false` with the pinned cert as the trust anchor.

    (B) Plain HTTP with cleartext scoped by the app, not the OS. CHOSEN.
        - Over Tailscale the traffic is already WireGuard-encrypted below HTTP;
          TLS on top would be redundant.
        - Over LAN the plan accepts cleartext as a 1.6 limitation:
          `remote.allow_lan` is off by default and the desktop shows a warning
          when it is switched on.
        - The enforcement point for "never talk cleartext to the internet"
          moves into the client: `client/src/core/remote/endpoint-probe.ts`
          (`hostClass` / `isAllowedRemoteHost`) refuses to pair with or probe
          any `http://` endpoint whose host is not loopback, RFC1918,
          link-local, CGNAT 100.64/10, or a `.local` / `.ts.net` / single-label
          name. The engine only ever publishes addresses from those ranges.

  RESIDUAL RISK (accepted, not introduced here)
  A device on the same LAN as a desktop with `allow_lan=true` could observe or
  tamper with the plaintext HTTP between phone and desktop. Mitigations: LAN
  is off by default, the desktop warns when it is enabled, the per-device
  bearer token still gates every request, and the tailnet path is encrypted.
  A malicious page cannot exploit the permissive base policy either: the
  WebView only loads the bundled app, and every engine request goes through
  the app's own client code with the address checks above.

  The explicit entries below are kept as documentation of the always-on
  cleartext hosts (they were the whole allow-list before F10-28 and stay the
  same when the base policy is tightened again in 1.7).
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The embedded engine (F10-9/F10-10), always loopback. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">::1</domain>
        <!-- Android emulator's alias for the host machine's loopback -
             used by the CI emulator-smoke job and local dev to reach a
             desktop engine started with `remote.allow_lan` (see
             PLAN-1.6-MOBILE.md section 8). -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
